      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To investigate the relationship between left ventricular diastolic dysfunction and episodes of angina decubitus."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The study population consisted of three groups, groups I (20 controls) had no cardiovascular diseases. Group II (20 patients) had coronary artery disease (CAD) without angina decubitus. Group III (24 cases with ejection fraction (EF) > 45%) were selected from 26 patients with angina decubitus."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Left ventriculography (LVG) showed that left ventricular (LV) first 1/3 filling fraction (1/3FF) was significantly reduced in groups III as compared with groups II and I (P < 0.01). Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (LVEDP) significantly increased in patients with angina decubitus after LVG as compared with that before LVG (P < 0.01). However, there were no statistically differences in LVEDP, before and after LVG in both groups II and I."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Patients with angina decubitus have abnormalities of LV diastolic filling and decrease in LV compliance."
        }
      ]},
